Cell["\<\
12:  Plot the parametric equation:  {cos(7 \[Theta]) cos(11 \[Theta]), cos(7 \
\[Theta]) sin(11 \[Theta])}  for  0 \[LessEqual] \[Theta] \[LessEqual] 2\[Pi] \
 so that it has Thickness 1% of the overall graph size and an aspect ratio of \
1.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.3903853722469597`*^9, 3.3903853832819595`*^9}, {
  3.390797386642*^9, 3.390797386731*^9}, {3.3907977373529997`*^9, 
  3.390797737427*^9}}],

Cell[TextData[{
 "13:  Many times it is necessary to use several graphic representations to \
determine the behavior under analysis.  This is especially true if the \
behavior to be observed is highly complex.  For example, say that you want to \
understand the behavior of the function:  sin( x / sin(y) )  for:   - \[Pi] \
\[LessEqual] x \[LessEqual] \[Pi],  - \[Pi] \[LessEqual] y \[LessEqual] \
\[Pi]. \n\nPart 1: As a first attempt, generate a 3D plot of this function. \
(Why might there be problems generating this graph when ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"y", "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"0", " ", "or", " ", "for", " ", "y"}], "=", "\[Pi]"}]}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 " etc.?)\n  \nPart 2: Generate a 3D plot of this function where the \
PlotPoints are set to 20. (More resolution is needed.)\n  \nPart 3: Generate \
a 3D plot of this function where the PlotPoints are set to 50 and the Mesh to \
False. (More resolution is needed.)\n  \nPart 4: Generate a 3D plot of this \
function where the PlotPoints are set to 200 and the Mesh set to False. \
(Eventually, either the computational time will become excessive or the \
computer will prove to be inadequate.)\n\nPart 5: Generate a density plot of \
this function with the Mesh set to False. (Why might there be problems \
generating this graph?)\n\nPart 6:  Repeat Part 2 for the density plot.\n\n\
Part 7:  Repeat Part 3 for the density plot.\n\nPart 8:  Repeat Part 4 for \
the density plot. \n\nPart 9:  Looking at the above 3D plots and the density \
plots, describe (in a Text Cell) the behavior of the \n             function  \
sin( x / sin(y) )  for:   - \[Pi] \[LessEqual] x \[LessEqual] \[Pi],  - \[Pi] \
\[LessEqual] y \[LessEqual] \[Pi]. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.39038538650496*^9, {3.39038595863896*^9, 3.39038601192896*^9}, 
   3.3903860506119595`*^9, {3.39038619383496*^9, 3.39038627523396*^9}, {
   3.39038640034296*^9, 3.3903864029569597`*^9}, {3.390730211624*^9, 
   3.390730229841*^9}, {3.390730353894*^9, 3.390730412355*^9}, {
   3.39073480704*^9, 3.390734859928*^9}, {3.390797389554*^9, 
   3.3907973896359997`*^9}, {3.3907977433859997`*^9, 3.390797743484*^9}}],

Cell[TextData[{
 "14: Using Manipulate[ ... ]:\n\nPart 1: Create a dynamic 2-D plot of:\n\n\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"-", "a"}], " ", "x"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 "  where  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"0", "\[LessEqual]", "x", "\[LessEqual]", "1", " "}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 "\n\tWith a ",
 StyleBox["continuous",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " slider for  a  where  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"0", "\[LessEqual]", "a", "\[LessEqual]", "10"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 "\n\t\nPart 2: Note that the plot jumps around as you move the slider.  Add \
a PlotRange option to your plot \n            (that plots All of the range) \
to fix this problem.\n\nPart 3: Create a dynamic 3-D plot of:\n\n\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"-", "a"}], " ", "x"}], "-", 
     RowBox[{"b", " ", "y"}]}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 "  where  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"0", "\[LessEqual]", "x", "\[LessEqual]", "1", " "}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 " and  0 \[LessEqual] y \[LessEqual] 1 \n\tWith a ",
 StyleBox["continuous",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " slider for  a  where  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"0", "\[LessEqual]", "a", "\[LessEqual]", "10"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 "\n\tWith a ",
 StyleBox["discrete",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " slider for  b  where  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"0", "\[LessEqual]", "b", "\[LessEqual]", "10"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 "  in steps of 2"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.39038661270596*^9, 3.39038666806696*^9}, {
   3.39038687211796*^9, 3.3903869916539598`*^9}, {3.39038703139196*^9, 
   3.3903870853149595`*^9}, {3.39038756030496*^9, 3.39038758131696*^9}, {
   3.39038763079796*^9, 3.3903876330299597`*^9}, 3.3903914264769597`*^9, {
   3.390734939565*^9, 3.390734945311*^9}, {3.390776836082*^9, 
   3.390776911248*^9}, {3.39079739723*^9, 3.390797397356*^9}, {
   3.390797750583*^9, 3.390797750663*^9}}],

Cell[TextData[{
 "15: Using Manipulate[ ... ]:\n\n      Create a dynamic algebraic calculator \
that expands  (i.e.  Expand[ ... ]) the expression:\n\n\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox[
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"x", "+", "y"}], ")"}], "n"], TraditionalForm]]],
 "  \n\t\n      With a ",
 StyleBox["discrete",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " slider for  n  where  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"0", "\[LessEqual]", "n", "\[LessEqual]", "10"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 "  in steps of 1"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.39038661270596*^9, 3.39038666806696*^9}, {
  3.39038687211796*^9, 3.3903869916539598`*^9}, {3.39038703139196*^9, 
  3.3903870853149595`*^9}, {3.39038756030496*^9, 3.39038758131696*^9}, {
  3.39038763079796*^9, 3.3903876330299597`*^9}, {3.3903914264769597`*^9, 
  3.39039145662596*^9}, {3.3903914867819595`*^9, 3.3903916333009596`*^9}, {
  3.39039174024496*^9, 3.3903917560819597`*^9}, {3.3903918654959598`*^9, 
  3.39039186591496*^9}, {3.390730243856*^9, 3.390730257472*^9}, {
  3.390797402816*^9, 3.39079740287*^9}, {3.3907977582860003`*^9, 
  3.3907977583780003`*^9}}],

Cell[TextData[{
 "16. Analyze the behavior of the equation:\n\n\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"2", 
      SuperscriptBox["x", "2"]}], " ", "+", 
     RowBox[{"3", 
      SuperscriptBox["x", "3"], " ", "y"}]}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"4", 
      SuperscriptBox["x", "4"]}], "+", 
     RowBox[{"5", "y"}], "+", "6"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "\n\nPart 1: Solve (i.e. use Solve[ ... ]) the above equation for  y  in \
terms of  x.\n\nPart 2: Use the results from Part 1 to define a function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{"f", 
     RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "  where  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"y", "=", 
    RowBox[{"f", 
     RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ".\n            Remember, you have an expression for  y  in terms of  x  \
from Part 1.\n\nPart 3: Plot  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", 
    RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "  for  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"-", "4"}], "\[LessEqual]", "x", "\[LessEqual]", "4"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 ".\n\nPart 4: Calculate  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"f", "'"}], 
    RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " .\n\nPart 5: Plot  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"f", "'"}], 
    RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "  for  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"-", "4"}], "\[LessEqual]", "x", "\[LessEqual]", "4"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 ".\n\nPart 6: Find the critical points for  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", 
    RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "  by solving (i.e. use Solve[ ... ]) the equation\n\n\t\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"f", "'"}], 
     RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}], "=", "0"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "\n\nfor  x .  Note that the result is not very useful in this form (i.e. \
the answer contains Root[ ... ] objects).\n \nPart 7: Find the critical \
points for  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", 
    RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "  ",
 StyleBox["numerically",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " by solving (i.e. use NSolve[ ... ]) the equation\n\n\t\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"f", "'"}], 
     RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}], "=", "0"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "\n\n          for  x .  Note that the result has two complex and four real \
values.\n \n Part 8: Comment (in a Text Cell) on whether or not the four real \
roots found in Part 7 agree with the \n             plot in Part 5.  Also, \
which of the four real roots correspond to local maxima of  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", 
    RowBox[{"(", "x", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "?"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.390734991005*^9, 3.390735201507*^9}, {3.390735237369*^9,
   3.3907353176210003`*^9}, {3.39073561051*^9, 3.390735812318*^9}, {
  3.390735848177*^9, 3.390736203007*^9}, {3.390736279561*^9, 
  3.390736288457*^9}, {3.3907363208570004`*^9, 3.3907364892019997`*^9}, {
  3.390736642663*^9, 3.390736739125*^9}, {3.390736815766*^9, 
  3.3907369381549997`*^9}, {3.390737081999*^9, 3.39073710305*^9}, {
  3.390797406727*^9, 3.390797411932*^9}, {3.3907977617539997`*^9, 
  3.390797761889*^9}}],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\n\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   FrameBox[
    StyleBox[
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["Print",
       FontSize->16], 
      StyleBox[" ",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox["your",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox[" ",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox["homework",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox[" ",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox["exercises",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox[" ",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox["using",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox[" ",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox["the",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox[" ",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox["following",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox[" ",
       FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox[
       RowBox[{"steps", ":"}],
       FontSize->14]}],
     FontWeight->"Bold",
     FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]]], TraditionalForm]],
  FontSize->16],
 "\n\n \[GrayCircle] Just like you did for Lesson 1, at the top of your \
exercises, enter your name, your class and your quiz section into the top \
text cell as illustrated below:\n\n \[GrayCircle] Select ",
 StyleBox["only",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " the 2nd outer cell bracket containing ",
 StyleBox["ONLY",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " the ",
 StyleBox["exercises",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " section. (Do NOT print the lesson.):"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.390428545079*^9, 3.390428598755*^9}, {
   3.3904286397200003`*^9, 3.390429050267*^9}, {3.390429145796*^9, 
   3.390429147264*^9}, 3.39042929474*^9, {3.390429400382*^9, 
